hi there steve kaufman here um  finally finding some time to do some  videos i want to  continue in my series of faqs frequently  asked questions about language learning  today i want to talk about you know what  do you do when you feel stuck you're  losing interest you don't think you're  making any progress  what can you do  well i'm going to answer that and i'm  going to give you a very important  secret about language learning  and and it is this  the brain because you learn in the brain  okay you don't learn in the classroom  you don't learn from a teacher those  things are  relatively unimportant it's the brain  how do you feed the brain what do you do  to the brain to make it learn well the  brain likes two things okay  one in order to learn something you need  a certain degree of repetition  however the brain likes to be challenged  the brain likes novelty you have to  stretch it a bit okay if you are into  physical training and you always do the  same exercise you are not going to put  on any muscle  and i believe the brain is the same way  now i'll give you an example and then  i'll explain the example is this i've  started studying czech  and so i spent a fair amount of time on  check this past week just one week  i go back to russian and i understand  everything much more clearly you  understand that i went to another  language i gave the brain a new  challenge it's struggling to piece  together the check  doesn't understand much of it when i  feed it again russian it just eats it up  whereas when i was giving it a steady  diet of russian it was starting to get  i wouldn't say fed up but it wasn't  noticing things it wasn't as sharp so  you can do this in a number of ways you  can start on a second language as i did  with check or  simply when you're studying a language  and as you know i'm a great believer in  in using input of course if you can  speak if you got people to talk to go  for it but in the meantime there's so  much you can do on your own  and but the suggestion is vary it okay  vary the kind of content you're  listening to and also vary the degree of  difficulty don't just listen to  difficult stuff or you'll get frustrated  but don't just listen to simple stuff  either  very often the language teachers tell  you that you you should always be in the  sort of  you know n plus one this is krashen's  theory just slightly just a little bit  difficult for you or you should only  listen to read material where you know  95 of the words always in the sense of  making it easy for you  i say that's fine do some of that but  also  make sure you give yourself  a challenge go after some difficult  stuff and you know  i don't know if it's 50 50 70 30 but you  need to be regularly giving yourself  difficult stuff to do where you don't  know the words where you don't  understand it you close the book you  still don't understand it but you've  challenged your brain and when you go  back to that other stuff that's easier  for you you'll find it even easier okay  so when you're feeling stuck and you  don't think you're making progress  challenge your brain and when you're  learning mix it up don't just give it  the easy stuff give the brain difficult  stuff make it work train it toughen it  up so that when go it goes back to the  familiar it'll just eat it up there you  have it that's my frequently asked  question what do we do when we get stuck  thanks for listening bye
 

